#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:1
msgid "Undici Module vs. Node.js Built-in Fetch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:3
msgid ""
"Node.js has shipped a built-in `fetch()` implementation powered by undici"
" since Node.js v18. This guide explains the relationship between the "
"`undici` npm package and the built-in `fetch`, and when you should "
"install one versus relying on the other."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:8
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:10
msgid ""
"The `fetch()`, `Request`, `Response`, `Headers`, and `FormData` globals "
"in Node.js v18+ are provided by a version of undici that is bundled into "
"Node.js itself. You can check which version is bundled with:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:18
msgid ""
"When you install undici from npm, you get the full library with all of "
"its additional APIs, and potentially a newer release than what your "
"Node.js version bundles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:22
msgid "Keep `fetch` and `FormData` from the same implementation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:24
msgid ""
"When you send a `FormData` body, keep `fetch` and `FormData` together "
"from the same implementation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:27
msgid "Use one of these patterns:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:29
msgid "Built-in globals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:42
msgid "`undici` module imports"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:57
msgid "`undici.install()` globals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:59
msgid ""
"If you want the installed `undici` package to provide the globals, call "
"[`install()`](/docs/api/GlobalInstallation.md):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:77
msgid ""
"`install()` replaces the global `fetch`, `Headers`, `Response`, "
"`Request`, and `FormData` implementations with undici's versions, and "
"also installs undici's `WebSocket`, `CloseEvent`, `ErrorEvent`, "
"`MessageEvent`, and `EventSource` globals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:82
msgid "Avoid mixing implementations in the same request, for example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:106
msgid ""
"Those combinations may behave differently across Node.js and undici "
"versions. Using matching pairs keeps multipart handling predictable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:109
msgid "When you do NOT need to install undici"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:111
msgid ""
"If all of the following are true, you can rely on the built-in globals "
"and skip adding undici to your dependencies:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:114
msgid ""
"You only need the standard Fetch API (`fetch`, `Request`, `Response`, "
"`Headers`, `FormData`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:116
msgid "You are running Node.js v18 or later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:117
msgid ""
"You do not depend on features or bug fixes introduced in a version of "
"undici newer than the one bundled with your Node.js release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:119
msgid "You want zero additional runtime dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:120
msgid ""
"You want cross-platform interoperability with browsers and other runtimes"
" (Deno, Bun, Cloudflare Workers, etc.) using the same Fetch API surface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/best-practices_undici-vs-builtin-fetch.md:123
msgid ""
"This is common in applications that make straightforward HTTP requests or"
" in libraries that target multiple JavaScript runtimes."
msgstr ""

